Big O notation:
Each notation listed here has a coded example in the script: 
O(1) = memory/effort required is consistently the same
O(logn) = we put in effort logarithmically, therefore lesser
O(n) =  we go through each and every entry
O(n^2) = commonly in loop within loop, and wasteful memory wise, spends exponentially double
O(2^n) = doubles the effort/memory used exponentially every timer

Data structure = is how data is organized inside our systems/computers/phones
since date or raw information is in an enormous amount a proper organization is needed for efficiency
Data structure is a way of organizing data so that specific operations like insert/delete/etc are performed in the most efficient manner in terms of space and time allocation.
-> types= linear and non linear
LINEAR DATA STRUCTURE:
    1)array/list
    2)linked list
    3)stack
    4)queue

    ARRAY:
        possible operations= read, add, remove
        python has list which behaves like array but unlike array its dynamically allocated
        Array's indexes start from 0
            Access operation at any position cost O(1)
            Insertion/deletion at end is O(1)
            Insertion/deletion in the middle is O(n)
            Slicing is performed over a range of elements and is O(k) such that k<n 
        
    
